note bottom of BacktestRangeCoverage
  This package holds SIX more interfaces that are **not** on this
  diagram: IMarketDataRepository, ISymbolCatalogRepository,
  IExchangeClient, IExchangeSessionFactory, ILiveStreamService,
  ISymbolMarketMetadataCache. They live in contracts/ but are
  internal to the module — only the five above are bound in
  composition/port_bindings.py and reachable from outside.
  contracts/__init__.py's own table is the maintained list.
end note

note bottom of MarketDataSyncRequest
  **Each of the five ports also ships a second implementation**, in
  contracts/testing/: FakeHistoricalKlines, FakeSymbolCatalog,
  FakeMarketDataSync, FakeMarketStream, FakeRangeCoverage, plus a
  contract suite per port run against BOTH the service above and the
  fake (HLD 10.3). They are part of the PUBLISHED surface, not test
  scaffolding: a consumer's test imports the fake rather than mocking
  a foreign port, which HLD 10.3 rule 4 forbids outright.
end note

note bottom of IMarketStream
  owner_id is a NAMESPACE, not an exclusive lease: two owners may
  stream the same symbol, and one owner may hold several symbols in
  one set (Dev Board shows n charts). start() REPLACES that owner's
  whole set and never touches another owner's, which is what keeps
  BOT-126/BUG-085 (two screens fighting over one stream) from
  recurring. An empty symbols list is a ValueError, not a quiet
  no-op: an empty set is indistinguishable from a stopped stream.

  DISTANCE FROM THE SPEC (PR 1.1b, ADR D12).
  Specified: start(symbol, timeframe, owner_id) -> StreamHandle,
  stop(handle), stop_all(owner_id). Shipped: no handle. A handle is
  not a naming choice — it needs ILiveStreamService to hold
  per-stream subscriptions instead of replacing an owner's set,
  which is a behaviour change ADR D12 keeps out of a port PR. So
  today's stop(owner_id) is both stop(handle) and stop_all(owner_id).
  Cut the seam in Phase 2, where strategy wants one stream per armed
  symbol (architecture-rule 7.2.1: at the second consumer, not before).
end note

note bottom of IMarketDataSync
  DISTANCE FROM THE SPEC (PR 0.5, ADR D12).
  Specified: sync(symbol, timeframe, start, end, owner_id) -> SyncHandle
  wrapping the Engine's CancellationToken, plus cancel(handle).
  Shipped: one frozen request DTO in, None out, and cancellation as a
  caller-owned CancellationCheck callable polled between fetches —
  because the caller already owns cancellation (async-ui-action-rule)
  and the module must not learn the Engine's CancellationToken to read
  one bool. Returning None is deliberate, not unfinished: callers read
  the store or watch the events afterwards, and the handler this wraps
  has always returned None. SyncHandle does not exist in src/.
  days_back_if_empty is absent for the same reason as quote_asset
  below: it is in the old command and no caller ever passed it (HLD 2.4).
end note

note bottom of ISymbolCatalog
  DISTANCE FROM THE SPEC (PR 1.2).
  Specified: list_symbols(quote_asset : str | None). Measured, nothing
  filters by quote asset — the picker's tabs split the whole list in
  ui/components/symbol_picker/quote_asset.py, and no caller ever asked
  the module for a subset. Publishing the parameter would publish a
  filter nobody implements (HLD 2.4). What shipped is the flag the
  picker's manual refresh does pass (BUG-066). The module also exports
  normalised_symbols(symbols) — upper, trim, dedupe, sort — from this
  file rather than application/, so the fake normalises exactly as the
  real one does without importing across the layer.
end note

note bottom of IHistoricalKlines
  DISTANCE FROM THE SPEC (PR 1.1a).
  Specified: one load(symbol, timeframe, start, end). The handler it
  replaced returned list[MarketData] | dict[str, list[MarketData]],
  chosen by whether `symbol` was a str or a list — a union decided by
  an argument's RUNTIME TYPE, which architecture-rule 2.1 forbids. Two
  methods with one return type each replace it: only the Dev Board
  loads several symbols, and it wants them keyed by symbol. load_many()
  is separate because its implementation may fetch concurrently, which
  is the only reason a caller would ask that way instead of looping.
  Tuples, not lists: the rows are a snapshot (domain-truth-rule).
  newest_first selects WHICH rows a limit keeps, not just their order.
end note

note bottom of IRangeCoverage
  DISTANCE FROM THE SPEC (PR 1.2).
  Specified: coverage(...) -> RangeCoverageSnapshot. That class exists,
  but it is IMarketDataRepository's own answer — five aggregates the
  database computed — so reusing the name here would have pointed at
  the wrong type. BacktestRangeCoverage keeps its name: three screens
  already read every field of it to tell the user WHY a range is
  unusable (BUG-072), and renaming a DTO that crosses the edge is a
  behaviour-free churn ADR D12 has no room for. The port answers,
  never raises, for a symbol with nothing stored: is_fully_covered
  False with both open times None says "nothing here", which a screen
  must render differently from "some of it is missing".
end note
@enduml
